__d(function(e,a,t,o,n,i,r){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 History",textbook:"Our Pasts-I",chapter_number:2,chapter_title:"On the Trail of the Earliest People",total_mcqs:20,mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What did hunter-gatherers eat?",options:["A) Only meat from hunted animals","B) Only fruits and vegetables","C) Wild animals, fish, birds, fruits, roots, nuts, seeds, leaves, and eggs","D) Cultivated crops like wheat and rice"],correct_answer:"C) Wild animals, fish, birds, fruits, roots, nuts, seeds, leaves, and eggs",explanation:"Hunter-gatherers had a diverse diet from nature \u2014 they hunted wild animals, caught fish and birds, and gathered fruits, roots, nuts, seeds, leaves, stalks, and eggs. They did not farm or grow crops. Their diet was entirely dependent on what nature provided in the area where they were living at that time."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why did hunter-gatherers move from place to place?",options:["A) They enjoyed exploring new places","B) They were searching for better weather","C) Food resources got exhausted, animals migrated, and they needed water","D) They were following rivers to the sea"],correct_answer:"C) Food resources got exhausted, animals migrated, and they needed water",explanation:"Hunter-gatherers moved because: food ran out in one area; animals they hunted moved seasonally; different plants were available in different seasons; and they needed reliable water sources. This movement was strategic \u2014 driven by survival needs, not curiosity or weather preferences."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Where is the famous prehistoric cave painting site Bhimbetka located?",options:["A) Karnataka","B) Rajasthan","C) Madhya Pradesh","D) Maharashtra"],correct_answer:"C) Madhya Pradesh",explanation:"Bhimbetka is located in Madhya Pradesh, India. Its caves contain prehistoric paintings by early humans depicting animals, hunting scenes, and human activities. It was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2003. It is one of the most important sources of evidence about the lives of hunter-gatherers in India."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Where is Mehrgarh located?",options:["A) Gujarat, India","B) Rajasthan, India","C) Balochistan, present-day Pakistan","D) Afghanistan"],correct_answer:"C) Balochistan, present-day Pakistan",explanation:"Mehrgarh is located in Balochistan in present-day Pakistan, near the Bolan Pass \u2014 an important mountain route into Iran. It is one of the earliest known villages in South Asia, dating to approximately 8000 years ago. People there grew wheat and barley and herded sheep and goats."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which crops were first grown at Mehrgarh?",options:["A) Rice and millet","B) Wheat and barley","C) Cotton and sugarcane","D) Maize and sorghum"],correct_answer:"B) Wheat and barley",explanation:"Wheat and barley were the first crops grown at Mehrgarh about 8000 years ago. Rice was cultivated in different areas \u2014 north of the Vindhyas. Maize and sorghum are from other parts of the world. Mehrgarh's wheat and barley cultivation is among the earliest evidence of farming in South Asia."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which animals were herded at Mehrgarh?",options:["A) Horses and elephants","B) Cows and buffaloes","C) Sheep and goats","D) Pigs and dogs"],correct_answer:"C) Sheep and goats",explanation:"At Mehrgarh, the animals that were herded (domesticated) were sheep and goats. The presence of their bones alongside evidence of domestication (age distribution of bones suggests selective breeding) indicates herding. Wild animals like deer and pig were still hunted, showing the transition from hunting to herding was gradual."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"How many years ago did the settlement at Mehrgarh begin?",options:["A) About 4700 years ago","B) About 8000 years ago","C) About 12,000 years ago","D) About 2 million years ago"],correct_answer:"B) About 8000 years ago",explanation:"Mehrgarh was settled approximately 8000 years ago (around 6000 BCE). This is earlier than the Harappan cities (about 4700 years ago) but after the beginnings of domestication (about 12,000 years ago). Mehrgarh represents the transition from hunting-gathering to farming and herding in South Asia."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is a perennial water source?",options:["A) A water source that exists only in the rainy season","B) A water source available throughout the year","C) A source of drinking water in cities","D) A water source underground"],correct_answer:"B) A water source available throughout the year",explanation:"A perennial water source is one that has water all year round \u2014 even in summer and winter. Rivers like the Ganga, Narmada, and Indus are generally perennial. Hunter-gatherers preferred to settle near perennial sources because they could be sure of water all year. Seasonal water sources dry up in summer, forcing people to move."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What were stone tools used for by hunter-gatherers?",options:["A) Building permanent houses","B) Writing and record-keeping","C) Cutting, scraping, hunting, and preparing food","D) Trading with other communities"],correct_answer:"C) Cutting, scraping, hunting, and preparing food",explanation:"Stone tools served multiple purposes: hand axes for chopping; scrapers for processing animal hides and plant materials; blades for cutting; and microliths as parts of spears and arrows for hunting. Stone tools were the multipurpose 'technology' of hunter-gatherers \u2014 they had no metal, paper, or other materials to work with."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why did hunter-gatherers prefer to live in caves and rock shelters?",options:["A) Because they were afraid of forests","B) Because caves were cooler in summer","C) For protection from weather and wild animals without needing to build","D) Because they were ordered to by rulers"],correct_answer:"C) For protection from weather and wild animals without needing to build",explanation:"Caves and rock shelters provided natural, ready-made protection from rain, cold, and wild animals without any construction effort. Since hunter-gatherers moved frequently, building permanent structures was impractical. Natural caves were ideal temporary shelters. Many caves also had nearby water sources and access to hunting grounds."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Archaeologists found both wild animal bones (deer, pig) and domesticated animal bones (sheep, goat) at Mehrgarh. What does this tell us?",options:["A) People at Mehrgarh traded animals with each other","B) The transition from hunting to herding was gradual \u2014 both practices coexisted","C) Wild animals at Mehrgarh were tame enough to live with humans","D) Mehrgarh people were not good at farming"],correct_answer:"B) The transition from hunting to herding was gradual \u2014 both practices coexisted",explanation:"The presence of both wild animal bones (deer, pig \u2014 showing hunting) and domesticated animal bones (sheep, goat \u2014 showing herding) at the same site proves that hunting and herding coexisted at Mehrgarh. This is evidence that the transition from hunting to herding (and from gathering to farming) was not sudden but gradual \u2014 people adopted new practices while maintaining old ones."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Around how many years ago did grasslands develop, creating conditions for farming?",options:["A) About 2 million years ago","B) About 8000 years ago","C) About 12,000 years ago","D) About 4700 years ago"],correct_answer:"C) About 12,000 years ago",explanation:"Around 12,000 years ago, the global climate became warmer, leading to the development of grasslands. This environmental change produced new animals (cattle, deer) that lived on grass, and new grass species whose seeds could be cultivated. This change created the ecological conditions that made farming and herding possible, eventually leading to the agricultural settlements like Mehrgarh (8000 years ago)."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why are stone tools more useful to archaeologists than wooden tools from the same period?",options:["A) Stone tools were used more frequently than wooden tools","B) Stone tools are more durable and survive for millions of years, while wood decays quickly","C) Stone tools are larger and therefore easier to find","D) Wooden tools were considered more valuable and were buried with the dead"],correct_answer:"B) Stone tools are more durable and survive for millions of years, while wood decays quickly",explanation:"Stone is an inorganic, non-biodegradable material \u2014 it can survive for millions of years in soil without significant deterioration. Wood, leather, cloth, and other organic materials break down relatively quickly under most conditions. Therefore, while early humans certainly used wooden tools and weapons, almost none have survived. Stone tools are the most abundant and oldest physical evidence of human activity."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What were the new developments that came with the farming and herding way of life?",options:["A) Writing, mathematics, and metal tools","B) Pottery, weaving, permanent houses, and food storage","C) Coins, trade routes, and urban planning","D) Boats, fishing nets, and deep-sea fishing"],correct_answer:"B) Pottery, weaving, permanent houses, and food storage",explanation:"The farming lifestyle brought new developments: pottery (for cooking grains and storing food), weaving (using cotton and other plant fibres), permanent mud-brick houses (since people no longer moved constantly), and food storage (grain could be stored for lean seasons). These represent a fundamentally different way of organising daily life compared to the nomadic hunter-gatherer lifestyle."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which site in Karnataka provides evidence of early Stone Age and Neolithic human settlements?",options:["A) Hampi, Bellary","B) Brahmagiri, Hassan district","C) Halebidu, Hassan district","D) Badami, Bagalkot district"],correct_answer:"B) Brahmagiri, Hassan district",explanation:"Brahmagiri in Hassan district, Karnataka, is an important archaeological site excavated by Mortimer Wheeler in 1947. It revealed evidence of Neolithic settlements including stone tools, animal bones (sheep, goat, cattle), pottery, and evidence of a transition to iron use. It is one of the most significant early human habitation sites in South India. Hampi is a medieval site; Halebidu and Badami are medieval temple sites."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is a microlithic tool? How was it different from earlier stone tools?",options:["A) A very large stone tool used for heavy work","B) A tiny sharp stone blade used as part of a composite tool like an arrow or sickle","C) A stone tool used specifically for grinding grain","D) A tool made of a mixture of stone and wood"],correct_answer:"B) A tiny sharp stone blade used as part of a composite tool like an arrow or sickle",explanation:"Microliths (micro = tiny, lith = stone) are very small, sharp stone blades that were attached to wooden handles or shafts to make composite tools \u2014 several blades in a row to make a serrated sickle, or one blade as an arrowhead. They represent technological advance over earlier, larger stone tools because they were lighter, used less material, and could be combined in versatile ways."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Hard",question:"The chapter mentions that 'women and men first began to grow crops.' Why might women have played a particularly important role in the discovery of agriculture?",options:["A) Women were stronger than men in early communities","B) Women, as primary gatherers of plants, had the deepest knowledge of plant growth cycles and edible species \u2014 making them most likely to notice how plants grew from seeds","C) Men were too busy hunting to notice plant growth","D) Women were given the task of farming by community leaders"],correct_answer:"B) Women, as primary gatherers of plants, had the deepest knowledge of plant growth cycles and edible species \u2014 making them most likely to notice how plants grew from seeds",explanation:"In most hunter-gatherer societies, women were the primary gatherers of plants while men focused on hunting. This means women accumulated the most detailed knowledge of plant species, seasonal availability, growth patterns, and edibility. This knowledge would have been the foundation for noticing that seeds could be planted and crops cultivated. Many anthropologists and historians believe women played a central role in the agricultural revolution."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Hard",question:"Why is the location of Mehrgarh near the Bolan Pass historically significant?",options:["A) The Bolan Pass provided access to the sea and fishing","B) The Bolan Pass was an important trade and movement route connecting South Asia to Iran and Central Asia","C) The Bolan Pass had the richest soil for farming in the region","D) The Bolan Pass provided protection from river floods"],correct_answer:"B) The Bolan Pass was an important trade and movement route connecting South Asia to Iran and Central Asia",explanation:"The Bolan Pass in Balochistan is an important mountain pass connecting the subcontinent to Iran and beyond. Mehrgarh's location near this pass is significant because it was on a major movement and trade corridor. This position may have facilitated the exchange of crops, animals, and ideas between South Asia and West Asia, contributing to the spread of agricultural practices. Archaeological evidence at Mehrgarh shows connections with communities further west."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Hard",question:"How did the development of pottery change the lives of early farming communities?",options:["A) Pottery allowed people to build stronger houses","B) Pottery enabled cooking of grains, storage of food, and carrying of water \u2014 fundamentally changing diet and lifestyle","C) Pottery was used to create weapons for hunting","D) Pottery was only used for religious rituals"],correct_answer:"B) Pottery enabled cooking of grains, storage of food, and carrying of water \u2014 fundamentally changing diet and lifestyle",explanation:"Pottery was transformative for farming communities: grains like wheat, rice, and lentils could be cooked into porridge and bread \u2014 making them major dietary staples (hard to cook without pots); food could be stored for lean seasons, reducing the risk of starvation; water could be carried and stored. Pottery essentially unlocked the full nutritional and storage potential of the farming lifestyle, making settled life much more viable."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Hard",question:"A student argues: 'Hunter-gatherers were primitive because they didn't farm.' How would you use the chapter's content to challenge this view?",options:["A) The student is right \u2014 not farming makes hunter-gatherers less advanced","B) Hunter-gatherers had sophisticated knowledge of hundreds of plant and animal species, made complex stone tools, understood seasons and landscapes deeply, and lived successfully for over 2 million years \u2014 this requires great intelligence and skill","C) Hunter-gatherers were actually more advanced because they had more freedom than farmers","D) The question of 'primitive' or 'advanced' is irrelevant to history"],correct_answer:"B) Hunter-gatherers had sophisticated knowledge of hundreds of plant and animal species, made complex stone tools, understood seasons and landscapes deeply, and lived successfully for over 2 million years \u2014 this requires great intelligence and skill",explanation:"Calling hunter-gatherers 'primitive' misunderstands their capabilities. They possessed extraordinary knowledge of their environments \u2014 knowing which plants were edible, which were medicinal, which were toxic; tracking animal movements; predicting weather; navigating landscapes. Their stone tool technology grew increasingly sophisticated over millennia. Most importantly, the hunter-gatherer lifestyle supported human survival for over 2 million years \u2014 an extraordinary record of success. The farming lifestyle is not inherently 'better' \u2014 it is simply different, suited to different environmental conditions."}]}},2728,[]);